course: dictionary
lesson: 522
title: Asteroids: rocks in space
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An asteroid is a rocky or metal object in space. It is smaller than a planet and goes around the Sun. Context helps you check this: «Most known asteroids orbit in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter» tells you asteroids orbit, and orbit means travel around something.
explain: Asteroids can be very different sizes. «Asteroids are the leftover building blocks from when the planets formed, lumps of rock and metal from pebble size up to hundreds of kilometres across.» The clue is the word from pebble size up to, which shows the range runs from very small to very big.
explain: Some words in space science are close in meaning but not the same. An asteroid stays in space. A meteor is a small piece that falls through the air and glows. A meteorite is a piece that lands on the ground. Check the sentence to see which one fits.
fact: An asteroid is a rocky or metal object in space that goes around the Sun.
fact: Asteroids are smaller than planets, and they range from the size of a pebble to hundreds of kilometres across.
fact: Most known asteroids are in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, and more than a million have been catalogued.
fact: All the asteroids in the belt together weigh only about 3 percent of our Moon.
fact: Scientists track big asteroids, and none of the known large ones will hit Earth in the next hundred years.
exercise: «Most known asteroids orbit in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter.» Where are most known asteroids? | Inside Jupiter | *Between Mars and Jupiter | Between Earth and the Moon | On the surface of the Sun
exercise: «Asteroids are the leftover building blocks from when the planets formed.» What does leftover suggest? | Made of water and ice | Brand new and shiny | Huge and heavy | *Left over from long ago, not used up
exercise: A small piece from space that falls through the air and glows is called a | planet | moon | comet | *meteor
test: «Scientists track big asteroids, and none of the known large ones will hit Earth in the next hundred years.» What does track mean here? | Paint with bright colours | Ignore completely | Throw away | *Follow and watch closely
test: What size can asteroids be? | Only as big as a house | Bigger than the Sun | *From a pebble to hundreds of kilometres across | Only the size of a car
exam: What is an asteroid? | A tiny sea animal | A kind of cloud made of gas | A star that burns very hot | *A rocky or metal object in space, smaller than a planet, that goes around the Sun
